A||||10||Ozzy||Whining noise in rear???||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||04-26-2000||11:20 PM||||Hi Humans!<P>My '88 Coupe has developed a loudish whining noise in the rear end of the car. It sounds just like heavy tread tyres and alters in level with vehicle speed. I jacked up the rear and placed it on jack stands. Then had my nephew run the wheels in 2nd gear and listened. I could hear nothing. I checked to see if the wheels had any bearing movement and can detect no major movement at all.<P>Could it be a wheel bearing that only makes the noise with wheight on it? <P>Any ideas much appreciated.<P>Ozzy||||reg||5
Z||000001||Shiner||04-26-2000||11:31 PM||||Maybe the horsepower fairy came and installed a supercharger while you were sleeping?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||04-27-2000||03:02 AM||||This may be a dumb question because I hope you checked it first... the tranny fluid level. If it's low, I wouldn't even bother with it, I will take it off your hands so you don't have to go through the hassle of adding some fluid. Ain't I a swell guy?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||04-27-2000||04:11 AM||||<BR>Ozzy, any chance that the whining noise in the rear is coming from your wife/ladyfriend in the back seat?<P>Oh, that's right, there IS no back seat!<P>No wonder she's whining... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000004||Gokart||04-27-2000||05:51 AM||||Cut down your bean intake!||||reg||11
Z||000005||Linx||04-27-2000||08:58 AM||||I had a whine, too.  Did the same- jacked it up and couldnt find it.  It <I>was</I> the wheel bearing.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||04-27-2000||10:39 AM||||Bearing noise is most apparent when it's under a load. Pull the rear wheels off and spin the bearings, there should be no play and the bearings should turn smoothly without any catching. Of course trannies have bearings too. Most people describe bearing noise as a whine or a growl that slightly changes pitch with speed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||04-27-2000||12:37 PM||||If the rear toe setting is off, it can cause your tires to wear unevenly.<BR>Are your tires "cupped"? If so, they can sound like truck tires (or a bad bearing). <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 04-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Homicidal||04-28-2000||12:22 AM||||I had a whining noise, it was a belt.  Whatever one is on the right side of the engine.<P>Then it snapped.  Opps!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||cobra427||04-28-2000||01:05 AM||||Here is something to think about when checking your rear wheel bearings.If you jack up your car and support it by jack stands, be sure that you place them under the A-arms.You only need to do this if you are going to rotate the wheels while the engine is running and in gear.Just allowing the wheels to "hang" down, could cause damage to your CV joints due to the acute angle that they assume.<P>Just a thought.<P>cobra427||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ozzy||04-28-2000||02:14 AM||||Apreciate the info gents...Have not had time to get back to it yet. The toe alignment thing is an interesting thought too. As is the fact of allowing the wheels to 'hang' when running the rear end of the ground.<P>I'm going to look at the wheel bearing possibility a little closer first. I will try to check the toe alignment as well.<P>thnx<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
